<h2>1. Supplier Selection and Verification</h2>
<p>The first and most critical step in importing steel structures from China is selecting the right supplier. With many steel structure companies in China, distinguishing genuine manufacturers from trading companies is essential.</p>
<p>Key verification steps include:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Factory audit</strong> — visit the factory or request a virtual tour to verify production capacity, equipment, and quality control systems.</li>
  <li><strong>Business license verification</strong> — check the company's registration, scope of business, and years in operation.</li>
  <li><strong>Export experience</strong> — ask for references from previous export projects, especially in your country or region.</li>
  <li><strong>Certifications</strong> — verify ISO 9001 and any country-specific certifications that apply to your project.</li>
  <li><strong>Production capability</strong> — confirm the manufacturer has the equipment, workforce, and capacity to handle your project scope.</li>
</ul>
<p>ZhongSai Steel Structure has 15+ years of export experience, a 100,000 m² production base, 100,000 tonnes annual capacity, and 300+ frontline workers. We welcome factory visits and provide complete transparency about our operations and project references.</p>

<h2>2. Quotation and Technical Specification</h2>
<p>Once you have selected potential suppliers, the next step is obtaining accurate quotations. Steel structure pricing depends on many factors, and providing complete technical information ensures accurate quotes.</p>
<p>Required information for an accurate quotation includes:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Structural drawings or architectural plans</li>
  <li>Bill of Quantities (BOQ) if available</li>
  <li>Building dimensions: length × width × eave height</li>
  <li>Project type and intended use</li>
  <li>Crane requirements (tonnage and lifting height if needed)</li>
  <li>Local climate data: wind speed, snow load, seismic zone</li>
  <li>Cladding and insulation requirements</li>
  <li>Destination port or country for shipping calculation</li>
</ul>
<p>A reliable manufacturer will review your drawings and technical requirements before providing a detailed quotation. Be cautious of prices that seem unusually low without a thorough technical review.</p>

<h2>3. Contract, Payment, and Production</h2>
<p>After confirming the quotation and technical scope, the contract phase begins. A clear contract should cover scope of supply, technical specifications, payment terms, production schedule, packing requirements, and delivery conditions.</p>
<p>During production, reputable manufacturers provide:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Production schedule and milestone updates</li>
  <li>Material traceability records</li>
  <li>In-process quality inspection reports</li>
  <li>Progress photos or videos</li>
  <li>Final inspection before packing</li>
</ul>
<p>ZhongSai follows a structured fabrication process covering cutting, welding, assembly, surface treatment, and final inspection. All primary members are marked with part numbers matching erection drawings for efficient on-site assembly.</p>

<h2>4. Shipping, Documentation, and Export Coordination</h2>
<p>Proper packing and container loading are critical for protecting steel components during ocean transit. Standard practices include:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Bundle packing for purlins, girts, and small sections</li>
  <li>Individual wrapping for large columns and beams with waterproof protection</li>
  <li>Wooden spacers to prevent shifting during transit</li>
  <li>Part number labels matching erection drawings</li>
  <li>Hardware sorted by connection point and packed in labeled boxes</li>
</ul>
<p>Export documentation typically includes commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading, certificate of origin, and material test reports. Freight forwarding and customs-clearance arrangements are coordinated with relevant service providers according to project requirements. ZhongSai is a steel structure manufacturer and export supplier, not a licensed customs broker or international freight forwarder unless explicitly confirmed for a specific project.</p>

<h2>5. Installation Technical Guidance and After-Sales Support</h2>
<p>After the steel components arrive at the destination, local erection and construction are handled by the customer or local project team. ZhongSai may provide overseas installation technical guidance according to the confirmed project scope, including:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Erection drawings and assembly sequences</li>
  <li>Technical support during installation</li>
  <li>Guidance on foundation bolt alignment</li>
  <li>Assistance with missing or damaged parts resolution</li>
</ul>
<p>Local construction, site labor, and statutory approvals remain the responsibility of the customer or local project team unless a specific written agreement states otherwise.</p>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>How long does production take?</h3>
<p>Production time varies by project size, complexity, and current production schedule. A typical standard warehouse may take several weeks, while larger or more complex projects require more time. Your supplier should provide a production schedule after confirming the technical scope.</p>

<h3>Can I use my own freight forwarder?</h3>
<p>Yes, many buyers use their own freight forwarder for ocean shipping. The manufacturer handles packing, container loading at the factory, and export documentation coordination. Confirm the arrangement in advance to ensure smooth handover.</p>

<h3>What payment terms are common?</h3>
<p>Common terms include a deposit with order confirmation and balance payment before shipment. Specific terms vary by supplier and project size and should be clearly stated in the contract.</p>

